The SMAP satellite datasets contain 55 different multivariate channels \cite{Hundman2018b}. Each channel file contains multivariate data, including the primary telemetry value and one-hot encoded commands (Figure~\ref{fig:smap_vis}). Our current approach focuses on extracting comprehensive temporal features from the primary univariate telemetry value.  SMAP datasets feature two main anomaly types: Contextual anomalies, where a point's deviation is only apparent given its surrounding context, and Point anomalies, where a data point distinctly deviates from the bulk of the dataset, regardless of context \cite{10400552}.

\section{Proposed Method}
\label{sec:proposed_method}

Our proposed methodology, termed Neural Contextual Anomaly Detection with Contextual Substitution (NCAD-CS), is a multi-stage methodology engineered to overcome the numerous complexities of anomaly detection in complex spacecraft telemetry. Building upon the foundational principles of contrastive learning from the original NCAD framework \cite{carmona2021neural}, we propose significant enhancements in feature representation, model architecture, context handling, and decision logic. These advancements are engineered to improve robustness against diverse signal behaviors, enhance sensitivity to transient and sustained anomalies, and ensure reliable performance in real-world operational scenarios. The core of our method remains the comparison between a time series segment (the \textit{suspect window}) and its preceding context. Still, several layers of adaptive intelligence now fortify this comparison. The overall workflow is depicted in Figure~\ref{fig:workflow}.

