\subsection{Dynamic Context-Aware Substitution and Scoring}
\label{subsec:substitution_scoring}
A critical failure mode in contrastive anomaly detection is \textit{context contamination}. As a sustained anomaly persists, it fills the context window, as illustrated in Figure~\ref{fig:context_suspect_contaminated}. A naive model comparing this contaminated context to the anomalous suspect window will perceive them as similar, causing the anomaly score to be suppressed and the fault to be missed.

To fortify our framework against this critical failure mode, we introduce a Contextual Memory Bank (CMB) and a dynamic, context-aware substitution mechanism. This mechanism ensures that the final anomaly score is always the result of a comparison against a trustworthy, normal baseline. The process is realized through the following stages:

\begin{figure}[t]
    \centering
    \includegraphics[width=\linewidth]{figure_3.png}
    \caption{Illustration of a clean context window (yellow) preceding the suspect window (red) at the onset of a contextual anomaly.}
    \label{fig:context_suspect_clean}
\end{figure}

\begin{figure}[t]
    \centering
    \includegraphics[width=\linewidth]{figure_4.png}
    \caption{Illustration of a contaminated context window, which can mislead standard context-vs-suspect comparison methods.}
    \label{fig:context_suspect_contaminated}
\end{figure}

\textit{1) Bank Construction with Raw Context Storage:} Our solution begins with constructing a memory bank as a stable, external repository of normal operational patterns. After initial encoder training, we encode all normal training context windows $\{w^{(c)}_{\text{train}}\}$ into a set of latent embeddings $\{z^{(c)}_{\text{train}}\}$. We then employ K-Means clustering, chosen for its computational efficiency, to partition this embedding space into representative clusters. The optimal number of clusters, $k_{\text{opt}}$, is determined through a principled evaluation of the silhouette score \cite{rousseeuw1987silhouettes, shahapure2020cluster} across a range of candidate cluster counts. The resulting $k_{\text{opt}}$ centroids \cite{uppada2014centroid}, $M = \{\bm{\mu}_1, \dots, \bm{\mu}_{k_{\text{opt}}}\}$, form the abstract prototypes of the memory bank. Critically, our implementation goes beyond storing only these abstract embeddings. For each centroid $\bm{\mu}_j$, we also identify and store the original, high-fidelity raw context window, $w^{(c)}_{\text{ref}, j}$, whose embedding is closest to it. This creates an essential dictionary, mapping abstract normal patterns to concrete, usable time series segments for substitution.

\textit{2) Establishing the Memory Bank Threshold ($\tau_{\text{MB}}$):} With the bank constructed, the next step is to define a quantitative gatekeeper for triggering the substitution logic. We compute the minimum distance from each normal training context embedding to its nearest prototype in the bank $M$. The memory bank threshold, $\tau_{\text{MB}}$, is then set to a high percentile (e.g., 99.0\%) of this resulting distance distribution. During inference, any context whose distance to the CMB exceeds this threshold is considered a candidate for substitution.
    
\textit{3) Dynamic Context-Aware Substitution and Scoring:} During inference, every test window undergoes a multi-faceted assessment before a score is computed. We calculate three independent confidence metrics: \textit{distance confidence}, based on the context's distance $d_{\text{context}}$ to the CMB; \textit{change confidence}, based on the embedding's dissimilarity to the previous context's embedding; and \textit{transition confidence}, based on the signal's internal variability. Three independent confidence metrics are calculated: distance confidence ($c_{\text{dist}}$), change confidence ($c_{\text{change}}$), and transition confidence ($c_{\text{trans}}$). These are then combined to produce the final \textit{substitution confidence score}, $S_{\text{conf}}$. The specific combination function used is the maximum operator, which allows the system to prioritize the most salient indicator for a given situation:
\begin{equation}
    S_{\text{conf}} = \max(c_{\text{dist}}, c_{\text{change}}, c_{\text{trans}})
\end{equation}
This approach ensures that a strong signal from any single metric, such as a large jump in the embedding distance from the CMB, is sufficient to flag the context as potentially contaminated and trigger further scrutiny.

The framework then executes its central, adaptive logic. The standard contrastive score, $S_{\text{orig}} = \mathcal{D}(z_{\text{test}}, z^{(c)}_{\text{test}})$, is always computed. However, if the context is flagged as contaminated (i.e., $d_{\text{context}} > \tau_{\text{MB}}$ and $S_{\text{conf}}$ meets a filter threshold), the substitution mechanism is activated. This involves querying the CMB to find the nearest normal prototype, $\bm{\mu}_j$, and retrieving its corresponding raw normal context window, $w^{(c)}_{\text{ref}, j}$. This known-good window is passed through the TCN encoder to generate a clean reference embedding, $z^{(c)}_{\text{ref}}$. A new, substituted score is then calculated: $S_{\text{sub}} = \mathcal{D}(z_{\text{test}}, z^{(c)}_{\text{ref}})$.

The final anomaly score for the window, $S_{\text{win}}$, is a confidence-weighted blend of the original score and a boosted version of the substituted score, ensuring a smooth but decisive transition from standard to substitution-based scoring:
\begin{equation}
S_{\text{win}} = (1 - S_{\text{conf}}) \cdot S_{\text{orig}} + S_{\text{conf}} \cdot (\beta \cdot S_{\text{sub}})
\end{equation}
where $\beta$ is a boost factor (e.g., 2.5) that amplifies the score when substitution is confidently applied. This mechanism, illustrated conceptually in Figure~\ref{fig:context_substitution}, ensures the anomaly score is always based on a comparison against a valid normal baseline, thereby preventing score suppression.

\textit{4) Context Substitution Indicator Map Generation:} Finally, after all windows are scored, we generate a point-level boolean \textit{context substitution indicator map}. Time points within the suspect regions of windows where context substitution was performed are marked as True. This map is not merely a diagnostic output but a critical data product that directly informs the final, robust thresholding stage.


