\subsection{Encoder Training}
\label{subsec:encoder_training}

The TCN encoder is trained using a contrastive learning objective \textit{before} the memory bank is constructed. This training aims to sculpt a discriminative latent space where embeddings of normal operational sequences are clustered tightly, while those containing anomalies are pushed distinctly apart. 

To achieve this, we programmatically inject synthetic anomalies into the 16-sample suspect window of a random 70\% of the training windows in each batch. The injected anomalies are sampled from diverse types, including spikes, level shifts, variance changes, and stuck-values, with magnitudes scaled relative to the context window's standard deviation. This process creates a challenging set of paired examples:
\begin{itemize}[noitemsep, topsep=0pt, leftmargin=*]
    \item \textit{Positive Pairs ($y=0$):} A normal window $w_i$ and its corresponding context $w_i^{(c)}$. The model should learn to produce similar embeddings for these, i.e., $z_i \approx z_i^{(c)}$.
    \item \textit{Negative Pairs ($y=1$):} A synthetically modified window $w'_i$ (containing an anomaly) and its original context $w_i^{(c)}$. The model should learn to produce dissimilar embeddings, pushing $z'_i$ far from $z_i^{(c)}$.
\end{itemize}

We employ a contrastive loss function with a fixed margin, $m$, which formalizes this objective \cite{le2020contrastive}. For a given pair of embeddings, the loss $\mathcal{L}$ is defined as:
\begin{equation}
\mathcal{L}(z_1, z_2, y) = (1-y) \cdot \mathcal{D}(z_1, z_2)^2 + y \cdot \max(0, m - \mathcal{D}(z_1, z_2))^2
\label{eq:contrastive_loss}
\end{equation}
where $\mathcal{D}(\cdot, \cdot)$ is the distance metric (e.g., Euclidean distance) and $y$ is the binary label indicating if the pair is positive ($y=0$) or negative ($y=1$) \cite{kostadinov2015vector}.

This loss function intuitively penalizes two distinct behaviors. For positive pairs, the loss is simply the squared distance, driving the model to make the embeddings of normal windows and their contexts as close as possible. The loss is zero for negative pairs if the distance exceeds the margin $m$. If the distance is less than $m$, the loss term pushes them apart until they are at least a distance $m$ from each other. Training on diverse synthetic anomalies makes the encoder highly sensitive to deviations from established temporal patterns.

\subsection{Final Inference and Thresholding}
\label{subsec:inference_thresholding}
The final stage of our framework translates the carefully computed window-level scores into decisive point-wise predictions through a principled, multi-step process.

\textit{1) Aggregation and Dynamic Weighted Smoothing:} The window-level scores $S_{\text{win}}$ are first aggregated into a point-level score sequence, $S_{\text{point}}$. We then apply a dynamic, weighted smoothing scheme. Two smoothed versions of the score sequence are calculated: one with a short-window simple moving average (SMA) (50 time steps) for responsiveness, and one with a longer window for stability \cite{hyndman2011moving}. The final smoothed score, $S_{\text{smooth}}$, is a weighted average of these two, where the weight for the long-window version is proportional to a smoothed version of the \textit{context substitution indicator map}. This intelligently lengthens the effective smoothing window during sustained anomalies, preventing premature score decay, while remaining highly responsive to isolated events.

\textit{2) Hybrid Dynamic Threshold Calibration:} A cornerstone of our method is the calculation of a channel-specific decision threshold, $T_{\text{final}}$, without hardcoded channel thresholds. We first leverage the \textit{context substitution indicator map} to isolate the subset of scores, $S_{\text{normal\_context}}$, corresponding to periods in which the context is not flagged for substitution. This produces a distribution-derived noise floor, $T_{\text{dist}}$, from the channel's own valid score distribution. In parallel, we generate a self-supervised calibration set from the training windows by applying the same contextual anomaly injection process used during encoder training. These synthetic clean and contaminated windows are scored by the full NCAD-CS pipeline, producing labeled calibration pairs $(s_i, y_i)$, where $s_i$ is a calibration score and $y_i \in \{0,1\}$ indicates whether the window was synthetically contaminated. We then select the calibration threshold $T_{\text{cal}}$ that best separates the synthetic clean and contaminated scores. The final threshold is chosen as a safeguarded hybrid threshold:
    \begin{equation}
        T_{\text{final}} = \max(T_{\text{dist}}, T_{\text{cal}})
        \label{eq:t_final_revised}
    \end{equation}
This design allows the decision boundary to adapt to each channel while preventing the synthetic calibration process from selecting a threshold below the channel's observed score-distribution floor.

\textit{3) Final Prediction with Temporal Consistency:} Finally, each time point $t$ is preliminarily flagged if $S_{\text{smooth},t} > T_{\text{final}}$. These flags undergo a temporal consistency filter to remove isolated, noisy detections. Crucially, a special condition allows extremely high-scoring points (e.g., $>1.8 \times T_{\text{final}}$) to bypass this filter, ensuring that critical, high-magnitude spikes are never inadvertently suppressed. Otherwise, points not part of a temporally coherent group are marked as normal ($\hat{y}_t=0$).

This end-to-end process, from context-aware substitution to robust thresholding, ensures that the final decisions are grounded in a reliable assessment of the system's normal operational state, effectively handling transient and sustained anomalies.


\section{Experimentation}
\label{sec:exp}

This section outlines the experimental methodology and assesses the performance of the NCAD-CS approach in detecting various types of anomalies.

\subsection{Experimental Setup}
\label{subsec:exp_setup}
The experiments were conducted using telemetry channels from the SMAP and MSL satellite dataset provided by NASA \cite{Hundman2018b}. The NCAD-CS model was configured with the following key hyperparameters, which were determined through empirical evaluation to balance performance and computational cost:
\begin{itemize}[noitemsep, topsep=0pt, leftmargin=*]
    \item \textit{Windowing:} A full window size of 300 samples was used, consisting of a 284-sample \textit{context window} and a 16-sample \textit{suspect window}. The windows were generated with a step size of 1.
    \item \textit{TCN Encoder:} The encoder was designed to produce embeddings with a latent dimension of 16.
    \item \textit{Memory Bank:} The CMB was constructed using K-Means clustering (Euclidean distance) \cite{kumari2016anomaly, chomboon2015empirical} on normal context embeddings. The optimal number of clusters was determined via silhouette analysis \cite{shahapure2020cluster} over a range of [1, 50]. The context normality threshold, $\tau_{\text{MB}}$, which gates the substitution mechanism, was set at the 99.0th percentile of distances from normal contexts to their nearest prototype in the bank.
    \item \textit{Training:} The encoder was trained for 40 epochs with a batch size of 32, using early stopping based on validation loss to prevent overfitting.
    \item \textit{Scoring \& Substitution:} The confidence-weighted context-aware scoring mechanism was used. A sophisticated substitution scoring logic was activated if a context's distance to the CMB exceeded $\tau_{\text{MB}}$ and met a minimum confidence filter. The final anomaly score was then a confidence-weighted blend of the original and substituted scores, amplified by a boost factor of $\beta=2.5$.
    \item \textit{Final Thresholding \& Smoothing:} Raw window scores were aggregated and then smoothed using the dynamic weighted smoothing scheme described in Section~\ref{subsec:inference_thresholding}. The final decision threshold, $T_{\text{final}}$, was selected by the hybrid dynamic calibration rule, which combines a distribution-derived score floor with a self-supervised calibration threshold obtained from synthetically contaminated training windows.
\end{itemize}


\subsection{Results and Analysis by Anomaly Type}
\label{subsec:results_analysis}
We analyze the model's performance across different anomaly types found in the SMAP dataset, using channels \textit{D-3}, \textit{A-7}, \textit{A-2}, \textit{P-3}, and \textit{A-1} as representative examples. This allows us to gauge performance across persistent contextual anomalies, subtle contextual faults, abrupt high-magnitude events, and isolated point anomalies.

\subsubsection{Persistent Contextual Anomalies (Regime Shifts \& Sustained Deviations)}
\label{ssubsec:persistent_contextual}
These anomalies represent prolonged deviations from the established norm. Channels \textit{D-3} and \textit{A-7} provide clear examples of such regime shifts.

\paragraph{Channel D-3 (Trend)} The latter part of channel \textit{D-3} (Figure~\ref{fig:d3_overview}, top panel) exhibits a clear upward trend starting around timestep 5200. As the signal trends away from the stable, flat baseline learned during training, the context windows become progressively more dissimilar to any pattern stored in the Context Memory Bank (CMB). Our framework robustly handles this scenario. The distance from the trending context embeddings to the normal prototypes in the CMB consistently exceeds the memory bank threshold ($\tau_{\text{MB}}$). Combined with high confidence from our dynamic weighting scheme, this activates the context substitution mechanism. As shown in the "Memory Bank Substitutions (Applied)" panel of Figure~\ref{fig:d3_overview}, context substitution is triggered throughout the trending anomaly. Crucially, for each contaminated window, it retrieves a known-good, flat context window from the CMB, generates a clean reference embedding ($z^{(c)}_{\text{ref}}$), and computes the anomaly score as the distance between the trending full window and this normal reference: $S_{\text{win}} = \mathcal{D}(z_{\text{test}}, z^{(c)}_{\text{ref}})$. This ensures the score remains consistently high. Furthermore, our dynamic smoothing logic identifies this as a sustained event and applies a long smoothing window, preventing score decay.

