\paragraph{Channel A-7 (Regime Shift to Simpler State)} Channel \textit{A-7} presents a different but equally challenging persistent anomaly: a regime shift from a complex, high-amplitude periodic signal to a simpler, low-amplitude, and relatively flat state (Figure~\ref{fig:a7_diagnostics}, top panel). This scenario tests the framework's ability to recognize that a simpler signal can also be anomalous. The effectiveness of this detection is rooted in the structure of the CMB itself, as visualized in the PCA plot of its embeddings in Figure~\ref{fig:a7_pca_bank}.

The plot reveals that the training data, consisting solely of the normal, complex periodic signal, is not represented as a single monolithic group. Instead, the TCN encoder and K-Means clustering have successfully partitioned the normal operational behavior into multiple clusters. Each colored cluster represents a specific, recurring pattern or \textit{phase} within the normal periodic signal (e.g., rising edge, peak, falling edge, trough). The centroids (red 'X's) serve as robust, learned \textit{prototypes} for these constituent normal patterns.

The normal signal is extremely noisy, triggering many contextual substitutions; however, when the signal transitions to a simpler state after timestep 6000, its context windows produce embeddings that are fundamentally different from any of these learned periodic prototypes. As no cluster in the CMB represents this new, simpler state, the embeddings are correctly identified as distant from all known-good patterns. This is why the "Distance to Memory Bank" in Figure~\ref{fig:a7_diagnostics} spikes and remains high. Consequently, the sustained abnormality triggers a high rate of context substitution, ensuring that anomalous full windows are consistently compared against valid periodic contexts from the CMB, leading to high and stable anomaly scores.

