\subsubsection{Subtle Contextual Anomaly}
\label{ssubsec:subtle_contextual}
Subtle contextual anomalies are characterized by short-duration deviations that still resemble a normal operational pattern in the immediate context. Channel \textit{A-2} is an excellent example of this category.

As shown in Figure~\ref{fig:a2_overview}, this channel exhibits a highly regular, periodic signal. A brief anomalous event occurs around timestep 4500, showing a subtle disruption in the signal's periodicity and amplitude. During such an event, the context windows are not sufficiently different from normal patterns to trigger substitution. Consequently, the scoring mechanism correctly defaults to the standard contrastive comparison, $S_{\text{win}} = \mathcal{D}(z, z^{(c)})$. The success of the detection then hinges on two other key components: 1) the sensitivity of the hybrid-pooling TCN encoder to capture the slight discrepancy, and 2) the robustness of the final threshold, $T_{\text{final}}$. Because our threshold is calculated from a pristine distribution of normal-context scores, it is not inflated by other events. It therefore remains sensitive enough to detect these subtle deviations, as the score peak clearly crossed the threshold.

