\subsubsection{Point Anomalies}
\label{ssubsec:point_anomalies}
Detecting isolated point anomalies, particularly on otherwise stable signals, presents a unique challenge. Channel \textit{A-1}, depicted in Figure~\ref{fig:a1_overview}, is a prime example, characterized by a near-constant signal baseline interrupted by a single, sharp negative spike.
The NCAD-CS framework successfully navigates this. The CMB, trained on constant-signal windows, creates a hyper-specialized cluster of normal embeddings. When the anomaly occurs, the encoder's \textit{Final State Pathway}, highly sensitive to recent changes, registers the spike's significant deviation. This produces a sharp, high-magnitude peak in the raw anomaly score. As shown in Figure~\ref{fig:a1_overview}, the resulting smoothed score peak decisively crosses the final adaptive threshold, and the final detection aligns perfectly with the ground truth.

