\section{Ablation Study}
\label{sec:ablation}

To quantify the contribution of the Context Memory Bank (CMB) to our proposed NCAD-TCN architecture, we performed a targeted ablation study. Our central hypothesis is that the CMB is the critical component for achieving high performance. We posit that it provides the necessary context to distinguish true anomalies from novel (but normal) operational states, thereby minimizing false positives and maintaining high precision.

For this study, we created an "ablated" model by disabling the CMB and all its associated logic (i.e., context matching, window substitution, and confidence scoring). In this configuration, the anomaly score is derived solely from the TCN autoencoder's reconstruction error, using a global percentile threshold. This effectively reduces our architecture to a standard, non-context-aware TCN autoencoder.

