\section{Conclusion}
\label{sec:conclusion}
This research directly confronted the critical failure mode of context contamination, where sustained anomalies blind conventional detectors by becoming the "new normal." Our NCAD-CS framework successfully overcame this challenge through its robust performance on the SMAP dataset. The model correctly identified persistent regime shifts (as in channels \textit{D-3} and \textit{A-7}) by using its Context Memory Bank (CMB) to prevent score suppression caused by context window contamination. Simultaneously, it retained the precision needed to flag subtle contextual faults (\textit{A-2}), abrupt events (\textit{P-3}), and isolated point anomalies (\textit{A-1}). This versatile performance is directly attributed to the synergy between a sensitive TCN encoder that detects deviations and an intelligent CMB-based scoring logic that validates the context, thereby ensuring a trustworthy and resilient anomaly detection system.

To quantify this performance against established methods, we present comparative results on the SMAP and MSL datasets. The key findings are summarized in Table~\ref{tab:recall_comparison} and Table~\ref{tab:f1_scores_all_models}.

