Performance comparisons further validate the effectiveness of NCAD-CS. As shown in Table~\ref{tab:recall_comparison}, our model outperforms the baseline LSTM in terms of contextual recall. This gain directly addresses the core challenge of sustained anomalies and context contamination, while maintaining a high point recall. This demonstrates the framework's versatility and specific success in its primary architectural goal.

Furthermore, the F1 score comparison in Table~\ref{tab:f1_scores_all_models} shows that NCAD-CS consistently achieves higher F1 scores than the original NCAD framework \cite{carmona2021neural} on both the SMAP and MSL datasets. This improved balance between precision and recall highlights the tangible benefits of our Context Memory Bank mechanism, which prevents score suppression and improves detection robustness in challenging, real-world telemetry scenarios.

It is important to note that the current framework constructs a static CMB based on an initial training period. While this approach ensures a stable and trustworthy baseline, protecting against the risk of the memory being contaminated by anomalies, it assumes a relatively stationary operational environment. For long-duration missions, this assumption may be challenged by concept drift, where the statistical properties of the telemetry data gradually change over time \cite{bhattacharya2022concept, almeida2023time}. Such drift can be caused by factors like gradual hardware degradation, evolving environmental conditions (e.g., changes in solar radiation), or the introduction of new operational modes not present in the initial training data. Over a mission's multi-year lifespan, a strictly static CMB could begin to misclassify this new, valid 'normal' as anomalous, potentially increasing false positives. Therefore, while our method is highly effective for its designed purpose, a key avenue for future research is the development of safe, online update mechanisms for the CMB, as outlined in our future work.

\section{Future Work}
\label{sec:future}
While NCAD-CS demonstrates significant advancements, several promising avenues for future research remain.  One includes an Autoregressive Context Prediction. Implement a predictive model (e.g., another TCN or LSTM) that learns to predict the expected context embedding $z^{(c)}_{\text{test}}$ based on past embeddings or raw context data \cite{bai2019compound}. The prediction error could serve as a complementary signal for identifying abnormal contexts.

